{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2864 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "1d468b9601b8338761f8e377c98b4a4daef74f4c5548c13d5647e6fe5cd014e6" :edb/db-before-t 2863 :edb/db-after-t 2864 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536176 50 #inst "2026-10-09T18:21:18.684Z" 13194139536176 true] [17592186129008 1100 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = t-\\sin t, \\quad y = 1-\\cos t, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,\\pi\\right]$.\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139536176 false] [17592186129008 1100 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx = t-\\sin t, \\quad y = 1-\\cos t, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,\\pi\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139536176 true] [17592186129008 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=\\pi$.\n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t - \\sin t) = 1 - \\cos t \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(1 - \\cos t) = \\sin t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}y\\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t)\\sqrt{(1 - \\cos t)^{2} + (\\sin t)^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t)\\sqrt{1 - 2\\cos t + \\cos^{2} t + \\sin^{2} t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 t +\\cos^2 t= 1\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t)\\sqrt{1 - 2\\cos t + (\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t)}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t)\\sqrt{1 - 2\\cos t + 1}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t)\\sqrt{2 - 2\\cos t}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t)\\sqrt{2(1 - \\cos t)}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t) \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - \\cos t}dt \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}π∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{(1 - \\cos t)^{3}}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536176 false] [17592186129008 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=\\pi.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $\\sin^2 t +\\cos^2 t= 1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536176 true] [17592186129009 1100 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = \\sin t, \\quad y = e^t, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,1\\right]$.\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?" 13194139536176 false] [17592186129009 1100 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx = \\sin t, \\quad y = e^t, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,1\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?" 13194139536176 true] [17592186129009 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1$.\n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t) = \\cos t \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{t}) = e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}x\\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}\\sin t\\sqrt{(\\cos t)^{2} + (e^{t})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}\\sin t\\sqrt{\\cos^{2} t + e^{2t}}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536176 false] [17592186129009 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536176 true] [17592186129010 1100 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = 1+3t, \\quad y = 2t^{3/2}, \\quad t