ore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}-t)={e}^{t}-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{e}^{t/2})=2{e}^{t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\sqrt{{({e}^{t}-1)}^{2}+{(2{e}^{t/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{t}+1+4{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression ${e}^{2t}+2{e}^{t}+1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\sqrt{({e}^{t}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\cdot |{e}^{t}+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\cdot ({e}^{t}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2t}+{e}^{t}-t{e}^{t}-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi [{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2t}+{e}^{t}-t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ using integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⇒ & v={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}x & =t{e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}-{e}^{t}.\\end{